      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The mechanism you proposed regarding the structured hydration shell of divalent cations like $Ca^{2+}$ is fundamentally sound, though the specific interplay between steric hindrance and hydration force repulsion requires careful calibration against empirical data from microporous soil matrices. The enthalpy of hydration for $Ca^{2+}$ is highly exothermic, creating a robust first hydration shell that acts as a kinetic barrier to approach the negatively charged phyllosilicate surfaces. This energetic penalty must be overcome by the lattice energy of the mineral surface or the release of competing water molecules, directly influencing the activation energy for adsorption. In the context of adsorption-desorption cycles, the high hydration enthalpy results in slower desorption kinetics compared to monovalent ions, effectively trapping the cation within the micropores until sufficient thermal energy or a change in ionic strength disrupts the hydration structure.\n\nHowever, the claim that this shell solely modulates approach via \"steric hindrance\" is an oversimplification; the primary driver is the thermodynamic repulsion arising from the overlap of hydration shells, which creates a disjoining pressure that opposes surface approach. Empirical studies indicate that in tight micropores, the confinement of",
